WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.750
Welcome to the story deck for
Building Global Secure Access,

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.525
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:07.525 --> 00:00:10.286
Instead of nine chapters one at a time,

4
00:00:10.286 --> 00:00:15.629
we'll follow one pilot ring all the
way through: a fictional agency tenant,

5
00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:21.150
contoso dot example, on November 16,
2026, and the same tenant two weeks later.

6
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.225
Every value comes from the
kit's own test fixtures,

7
00:00:24.225 --> 00:00:27.750
and every output on a slide is
what the kit really printed.

8
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:32.210
One note before we begin: the workbook
has been reviewed and approved,

9
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:37.740
and the code is illustrative, tested
against fixtures, not against a live tenant.

10
00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.028
Here's the route, in
the guide's run order.

11
00:00:44.028 --> 00:00:47.357
Scene one: the pilot
decides Internet Access on

12
00:00:47.357 --> 00:00:50.602
a feature whose GCC
availability isn't stated.

13
00:00:50.602 --> 00:00:53.427
Scene two: the logs leave a category out.

14
00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:56.540
Scene three: one device
is behind the rest.

15
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.133
Scene four: sign-ins
are short of the target,

16
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:01.577
and the first change goes through a plan.

17
00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:04.904
Scene five: the compliant
network policy isn't ready,

18
00:01:04.904 --> 00:01:07.302
and a partner tenant isn't registered.

19
00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:10.878
Scene six: a Finance app
that's really a VPN again.

20
00:01:10.878 --> 00:01:15.153
Scene seven: an expired bypass
and a certificate near expiry.

21
00:01:15.153 --> 00:01:18.252
Scene eight: the ring says no-go, then go.

22
00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:22.640
And scene nine runs the same
commands in test, then production.

23
00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.215
Scene one.

24
00:01:27.215 --> 00:01:30.301
The pilot's Internet
Access decision record

25
00:01:30.301 --> 00:01:33.053
looks fine at a glance: adopted, approved.

26
00:01:33.053 --> 00:01:37.258
But its quote of Learn's availability
sentence is forty-six days old,

27
00:01:37.258 --> 00:01:40.036
its SKU has twenty-five
units for sixty users,

28
00:01:40.036 --> 00:01:43.865
and it counts on the cloud firewall
for clients to handle QUIC.

29
00:01:43.865 --> 00:01:44.952
That's a preview.

30
00:01:44.952 --> 00:01:50.672
Learn says Global Secure Access is
available in GCC for the product as a whole,

31
00:01:50.672 --> 00:01:54.515
so a preview's GCC availability
is not yet specified.

32
00:01:54.515 --> 00:01:56.053
The kit doesn't assume it.

33
00:01:56.053 --> 00:02:01.278
Its availability table carries the guide's
interim path, and the check that catches

34
00:02:01.278 --> 00:02:06.720
the preview names it: block QUIC with a
Windows Firewall rule delivered by Intune.

35
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.195
Try it.

36
00:02:11.195 --> 00:02:14.932
From the kit's folder, check the
pilot's four decision records.

37
00:02:14.932 --> 00:02:17.921
Four failures: the internet
decision's stale quote,

38
00:02:17.921 --> 00:02:21.905
its short licenses and its preview
feature, and the branch decision,

39
00:02:21.905 --> 00:02:26.671
which defers remote networks without
naming what protects branches meanwhile.

40
00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:28.970
Then read the preview finding in full.

41
00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.133
Look at the fix.

42
00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:33.705
It isn't "enable the preview":
it's the guide's interim path,

43
00:02:33.705 --> 00:02:37.720
piloted first and recorded
in the architecture record.

44
00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:42.282
Check one.

45
00:02:42.282 --> 00:02:46.658
Learn says Global Secure
Access is available in GCC.

46
00:02:46.658 --> 00:02:49.442
So what does the guide's
table say about the

47
00:02:49.442 --> 00:02:52.307
cloud firewall for clients,
a preview, in GCC?

48
00:02:52.307 --> 00:02:54.482
Available, because the product is?

49
00:02:54.482 --> 00:02:55.857
Not yet specified?

50
00:02:55.857 --> 00:02:56.896
Not available?

51
00:02:56.896 --> 00:02:58.732
Or available after onboarding?

52
00:02:58.732 --> 00:03:01.140
The answer opens the next scene.

53
00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.410
The answer to check
one: not yet specified,

54
00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.115
because Learn's sentence covers
the product, not the preview.

55
00:03:11.115 --> 00:03:11.978
Scene two.

56
00:03:11.978 --> 00:03:14.611
The guide runs logging before the client,

57
00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:17.553
so the logs exist before
anything is enforced.

58
00:03:17.553 --> 00:03:22.601
The kit's collector reads the whole tenant
in one read-only run: seventeen calls,

59
00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.931
twenty-four reads, every file hashed,

60
00:03:24.931 --> 00:03:28.115
and later steps refuse a
collection that changed.

61
00:03:28.115 --> 00:03:30.984
The pilot's diagnostic
setting looks right but

62
00:03:30.984 --> 00:03:33.927
sends only three of the
four network categories.

63
00:03:33.927 --> 00:03:35.752
Remote network health is off.

64
00:03:35.752 --> 00:03:39.254
No branch is on a remote
network yet, but when one is,

65
00:03:39.254 --> 00:03:42.680
its health would never
reach the workspace.

66
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:47.155
Try it.

67
00:03:47.155 --> 00:03:49.818
Run the baseline checks
on the pilot collection.

68
00:03:49.818 --> 00:03:54.156
Both connector groups are in the
architecture record with two hosts,

69
00:03:54.156 --> 00:03:57.657
the forest is in the DNS
suffixes, the workspace is named,

70
00:03:57.657 --> 00:04:01.843
the export is complete, and the one
failure is the missing category.

71
00:04:01.843 --> 00:04:06.468
Then copy the collection, add one
space to one file, and try to read it.

72
00:04:06.468 --> 00:04:11.079
The read is refused: the licenses
file changed since its manifest.

73
00:04:11.079 --> 00:04:14.740
Every later chapter reads the
collection through that check.

74
00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:19.315
Check two.

75
00:04:19.315 --> 00:04:23.627
In the guide's run order, when is the
network diagnostic setting turned on?

76
00:04:23.627 --> 00:04:25.940
After the pilot, once there's traffic?

77
00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.052
Before the client, so the logs
exist before anything is enforced?

78
00:04:30.052 --> 00:04:32.603
At ring 3, with the CISA agreement?

79
00:04:32.603 --> 00:04:34.820
Or only when a finding needs it?

80
00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.285
The answer to check
two: before the client,

81
00:04:41.285 --> 00:04:44.320
so the logs exist before
anything is enforced.

82
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:45.370
Scene three.

83
00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:48.906
Every later policy assumes
the client is on the device,

84
00:04:48.906 --> 00:04:51.420
connected, current and hard to turn off.

85
00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:56.381
The service sees fifteen active devices,
but Intune says eighteen are in scope,

86
00:04:56.381 --> 00:05:00.732
so coverage is eighty-three percent
against a target of ninety-eight.

87
00:05:00.732 --> 00:05:04.349
One device, pilot 3, runs an old client,

88
00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:10.257
prefers IPv6, and Chrome
still uses DNS over HTTPS.

89
00:05:10.257 --> 00:05:12.220
Pilot 4 is disconnected.

90
00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:17.210
The kit's device script records each
device's values without changing anything,

91
00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.510
and hardening waits for
ring 2, as Learn advises.

92
00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:24.985
Try it.

93
00:05:24.985 --> 00:05:26.385
Run the client checks.

94
00:05:26.385 --> 00:05:29.410
Coverage fails at eighty-three
point three percent.

95
00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.028
Pilot 3 fails three
checks: the old client,

96
00:05:33.028 --> 00:05:38.348
IPv6 preferred, and DNS over
HTTPS left on in Chrome.

97
00:05:38.348 --> 00:05:41.159
Pilot 4 fails on health: disconnected.

98
00:05:41.159 --> 00:05:46.304
And the one client exclusion, a lab
machine with a Hyper-V external switch,

99
00:05:46.304 --> 00:05:50.248
passes, because it has an owner,
an approver and an expiry.

100
00:05:50.248 --> 00:05:54.340
The pilot isn't ready to exit,
and Scene eight will say so.

101
00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.002
Check three.

102
00:05:59.002 --> 00:06:03.042
Learn advises letting users run
the client unrestricted first,

103
00:06:03.042 --> 00:06:04.490
and restricting later.

104
00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.253
So what does the kit do with the four
hardening values during the pilot?

105
00:06:08.253 --> 00:06:10.315
Fail every device without them?

106
00:06:10.315 --> 00:06:13.552
Report them as information,
and check them from ring 2?

107
00:06:13.552 --> 00:06:15.027
Apply them automatically?

108
00:06:15.027 --> 00:06:17.320
Or ignore them in every ring?

109
00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:25.708
The answer to check three: information
in the pilot, a check from ring 2.

110
00:06:25.708 --> 00:06:26.683
Scene four.

111
00:06:26.683 --> 00:06:30.044
The Microsoft traffic
profile and signaling are on.

112
00:06:30.044 --> 00:06:34.907
Signaling is source IP restoration and
the signal Conditional Access needs.

113
00:06:34.907 --> 00:06:37.568
But only ninety-one percent
of the week's sign-ins

114
00:06:37.568 --> 00:06:40.095
went through the service,
against ninety-five.

115
00:06:40.095 --> 00:06:42.005
And the internet profile is on,

116
00:06:42.005 --> 00:06:46.283
though the pilot's desired state
hasn't adopted Internet Access yet.

117
00:06:46.283 --> 00:06:51.039
Fixing that is the first write, so it
goes through a plan: a file listing each

118
00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:52.520
request and its reason,

119
00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.770
with a hash a second person
approves before anything is sent.

120
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:01.245
Try it.

121
00:07:01.245 --> 00:07:05.072
Run the traffic checks: the
profile passes, signaling passes,

122
00:07:05.072 --> 00:07:08.745
and the sign-in share fails at
ninety-one point two percent.

123
00:07:08.745 --> 00:07:11.332
Then plan the pilot
from its desired state.

124
00:07:11.332 --> 00:07:16.149
Two Graph steps: the internet profile
goes off, and the administrator

125
00:07:16.149 --> 00:07:20.796
RDP policy goes back to its reviewed
file, which Scene six explains.

126
00:07:20.796 --> 00:07:25.058
The plan's hash is printed, and
it's a dry run: nothing changed.

127
00:07:25.058 --> 00:07:28.750
In fixture mode, applying
it is refused outright.

128
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:33.412
Check four.

129
00:07:33.412 --> 00:07:37.551
What does the plan script need before it
sends anything with the apply switch?

130
00:07:37.551 --> 00:07:38.663
Only the switch?

131
00:07:38.663 --> 00:07:43.338
The approved hash, a live collection and
a delegated session in the right tenant?

132
00:07:43.338 --> 00:07:44.862
A Global Administrator?

133
00:07:44.862 --> 00:07:46.550
Or a fixture folder?

134
00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.884
The answer to check
four: the approved hash,

135
00:07:52.884 --> 00:07:56.650
a live collection, and a delegated
session in the right tenant.

136
00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:57.675
Scene five.

137
00:07:57.675 --> 00:08:00.491
The compliant network
policy is built right,

138
00:08:00.491 --> 00:08:03.688
and it has been in report-only
for ten business days.

139
00:08:03.688 --> 00:08:07.165
In that time, forty-one sign-ins
would have been blocked from

140
00:08:07.165 --> 00:08:10.713
users off the service, which is
exactly what the policy is for.

141
00:08:10.713 --> 00:08:13.650
But three would have been
blocked for users on the service.

142
00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:17.450
Those are defects, and the
policy isn't ready to enforce.

143
00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:21.987
The plan won't enforce it without an
explicit switch the policy owner passes.

144
00:08:21.987 --> 00:08:24.823
And one partner tenant
is allowed by the tenant

145
00:08:24.823 --> 00:08:27.870
restrictions policy with
nobody's name on it.

146
00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:32.345
Try it.

147
00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:37.602
Check the compliant network policy: built
right, ten business days in report-only,

148
00:08:37.602 --> 00:08:41.132
and three failures carried by
the service, so not ready.

149
00:08:41.132 --> 00:08:45.542
Read the guide's report-only query:
eighteen hundred thirty successes,

150
00:08:45.542 --> 00:08:48.432
forty-one failures off
the service, three on it.

151
00:08:48.432 --> 00:08:52.771
Then plan ring 1, whose desired
state wants the policy enforced.

152
00:08:52.771 --> 00:08:56.465
The plan leaves it report-only
and says why on a manual line:

153
00:08:56.465 --> 00:09:00.360
enforcing needs the switch,
after the results are clean.

154
00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.048
Check five.

155
00:09:05.048 --> 00:09:07.494
In report-only, which compliant network

156
00:09:07.494 --> 00:09:10.311
failures are defects to
fix before enforcing?

157
00:09:10.311 --> 00:09:11.372
Every failure?

158
00:09:11.372 --> 00:09:14.672
Failures with through false,
users off the service?

159
00:09:14.672 --> 00:09:17.923
Failures with through true,
users the service carried?

160
00:09:17.923 --> 00:09:21.020
Or none, because report-only can't fail?

161
00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:28.045
The answer to check five:
failures the service carried.

162
00:09:28.045 --> 00:09:29.132
Scene six.

163
00:09:29.132 --> 00:09:33.419
Private Access is meant to give a
group one application on its ports.

164
00:09:33.419 --> 00:09:38.712
The pilot's Finance app is a slash sixteen
on every port, assigned to one person,

165
00:09:38.712 --> 00:09:43.095
under an enforced policy that
doesn't exclude emergency access.

166
00:09:43.095 --> 00:09:48.895
Every port includes RDP, so it's also
an administrative path with plain MFA.

167
00:09:48.895 --> 00:09:51.558
That's the VPN again, under a new name.

168
00:09:51.558 --> 00:09:53.922
The RDP app asks only for MFA too,

169
00:09:53.922 --> 00:09:58.469
and one cloud connector group
has a single active connector.

170
00:09:58.469 --> 00:10:02.957
The plan from Scene four brings the
RDP policy back to its reviewed file:

171
00:10:02.957 --> 00:10:06.900
the phishing-resistant strength
and a compliant device.

172
00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:11.375
Try it.

173
00:10:11.375 --> 00:10:13.288
Run the private access checks.

174
00:10:13.288 --> 00:10:16.759
The cloud connector group has
one of two connectors active,

175
00:10:16.759 --> 00:10:18.176
and the active one is old.

176
00:10:18.176 --> 00:10:22.855
Finance fails four ways: the wide segment,
the direct assignment, the missing

177
00:10:22.855 --> 00:10:25.603
emergency exclusion, and an administrative

178
00:10:25.603 --> 00:10:28.500
path without the
phishing-resistant strength.

179
00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.525
The RDP app fails the same strength check.

180
00:10:31.525 --> 00:10:34.200
And Quick Access still holds two segments.

181
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:38.260
Two weeks later, in the workbook,
every one of these passes.

182
00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:43.035
Check six.

183
00:10:43.035 --> 00:10:47.822
An application's segment
covers port 3389, RDP.

184
00:10:47.822 --> 00:10:51.034
What does the kit require of
its Conditional Access policy?

185
00:10:51.034 --> 00:10:51.973
MFA?

186
00:10:51.973 --> 00:10:56.134
The phishing-resistant authentication
strength and a compliant device?

187
00:10:56.134 --> 00:10:57.548
A trusted location?

188
00:10:57.548 --> 00:10:59.130
Or nothing extra?

189
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.705
The answer to check six: the phishing-resistant
strength and a compliant device.

190
00:11:07.705 --> 00:11:08.755
Scene seven.

191
00:11:08.755 --> 00:11:10.832
The pilot's web filtering works:

192
00:11:10.832 --> 00:11:15.385
the acceptable-use categories and
threat intelligence are blocked,

193
00:11:15.385 --> 00:11:20.019
and QUIC is blocked by the guide's
interim path, an Intune firewall rule,

194
00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:24.093
because the preview firewall's
GCC availability isn't stated.

195
00:11:24.093 --> 00:11:25.842
But two exceptions slipped.

196
00:11:25.842 --> 00:11:30.305
One bypass expired on November
first, and another has no approver.

197
00:11:30.305 --> 00:11:35.132
And TLS inspection is in trouble: the
certificate has seventy-four days left,

198
00:11:35.132 --> 00:11:40.342
inside the ninety-day renewal window, and
one and a half percent of sessions failed.

199
00:11:40.342 --> 00:11:44.480
The issuer, at least, is a
dedicated inspection authority.

200
00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:48.955
Try it.

201
00:11:48.955 --> 00:11:54.256
Run the Internet Access checks: the two
bypasses fail, and the QUIC check passes

202
00:11:54.256 --> 00:11:59.230
on the interim block, with the preview's
GCC state written into the finding.

203
00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:02.802
Then the TLS checks: the
certificate fails on both counts,

204
00:12:02.802 --> 00:12:05.995
the pinned payroll site's
bypass is the expired one,

205
00:12:05.995 --> 00:12:10.555
and the failure share is one and a half
percent against a threshold of one.

206
00:12:10.555 --> 00:12:13.506
Two weeks later the
certificate has been rolled,

207
00:12:13.506 --> 00:12:17.780
the bypasses renewed and approved,
and all thirteen checks pass.

208
00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:22.468
Check seven.

209
00:12:22.468 --> 00:12:25.468
Learn says an expired
inspection certificate

210
00:12:25.468 --> 00:12:28.468
disables all TLS inspection
across the tenant.

211
00:12:28.468 --> 00:12:30.554
When does the kit fail the certificate?

212
00:12:30.554 --> 00:12:32.067
On the day it expires?

213
00:12:32.067 --> 00:12:36.331
With ninety days or fewer left, or
less than six months' validity?

214
00:12:36.331 --> 00:12:38.255
Only after inspection stops?

215
00:12:38.255 --> 00:12:41.170
Or never, because renewal is manual?

216
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:48.551
The answer to check seven: with
ninety days or fewer left,

217
00:12:48.551 --> 00:12:50.683
or less than six months' validity.

218
00:12:50.683 --> 00:12:51.545
Scene eight.

219
00:12:51.545 --> 00:12:56.495
On November sixteenth, the pilot is
measured against the guide's exit criteria.

220
00:12:56.495 --> 00:13:02.175
Five business days in, one severity-two
ticket open, eighty-three percent coverage,

221
00:13:02.175 --> 00:13:07.020
three report-only defects, and one and
a half percent TLS failures: no-go.

222
00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:11.945
Two weeks later, ring 1 has every fix
from the last seven scenes in place.

223
00:13:11.945 --> 00:13:16.658
Every metric is met, every criterion
passes, and the ring says go.

224
00:13:16.658 --> 00:13:21.166
Before it moves on, its rollback is planned
in the guide's order, and every run is

225
00:13:21.166 --> 00:13:26.450
recorded as evidence that the Zero Trust
Assessment's closure register can point to.

226
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:30.925
Try it.

227
00:13:30.925 --> 00:13:33.525
Compute the pilot's metrics and exit.

228
00:13:33.525 --> 00:13:37.298
The failing criteria are on
the slide: time in the ring,

229
00:13:37.298 --> 00:13:42.700
the open severity-two, coverage, the
report-only defects, and the TLS failures.

230
00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:44.087
Pilot: no-go.

231
00:13:44.087 --> 00:13:46.662
Then the same for ring 1, two weeks later:

232
00:13:46.662 --> 00:13:51.568
time in the ring passes at ten business
days, coverage is a hundred percent,

233
00:13:51.568 --> 00:13:54.865
report-only is clean, the
emergency exclusion was

234
00:13:54.865 --> 00:13:58.530
tested on the ring's start
date, and the ring says go.

235
00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.067
Check eight.

236
00:14:03.067 --> 00:14:04.868
A ring is being rolled back.

237
00:14:04.868 --> 00:14:07.830
Where does turning signaling
off come in the guide's order?

238
00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.955
First, to stop the policies?

239
00:14:09.955 --> 00:14:12.955
Last, only after no policy depends on it?

240
00:14:12.955 --> 00:14:13.680
Never?

241
00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.490
Or together with tagging?

242
00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:23.678
The answer to check eight: last,
only after no policy depends on it.

243
00:14:23.678 --> 00:14:24.615
Scene nine.

244
00:14:24.615 --> 00:14:28.373
Ring 1 goes live, in a test
tenant first and then production,

245
00:14:28.373 --> 00:14:32.365
with one settings file per
environment and the same commands.

246
00:14:32.365 --> 00:14:37.124
Before any change, a read-only preflight
checks the stage, the collection,

247
00:14:37.124 --> 00:14:41.011
the emergency group's exclusion
from every network policy,

248
00:14:41.011 --> 00:14:43.390
the logs, and the approved decisions.

249
00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:48.134
The ring is enabled from a plan approved
by its hash, smoke-tested after its exit

250
00:14:48.134 --> 00:14:52.878
period, and rolled back from a plan written
before it started if a trigger fires.

251
00:14:52.878 --> 00:14:56.528
Production starts only when the
test stage's evidence passes.

252
00:14:56.528 --> 00:14:59.810
The runbook hasn't been
run against a live tenant.

253
00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:04.285
Try it.

254
00:15:04.285 --> 00:15:08.660
The runbook's own fixture run,
the fictional tenant at ring 1.

255
00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:12.476
The test preflight passes,
including the emergency exclusion

256
00:15:12.476 --> 00:15:16.072
from all five network policies
and the diagnostic setting.

257
00:15:16.072 --> 00:15:20.772
The smoke test runs a hundred checks,
none fail, and the ring says go.

258
00:15:20.772 --> 00:15:24.060
The test stage's evidence
forms an unbroken chain,

259
00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.509
with a passing record for
every step the stage needs.

260
00:15:27.509 --> 00:15:32.920
And so the production preflight's last check,
which reads that evidence, passes too.

261
00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:38.520
Check nine, the last one.

262
00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:43.320
An operator runs the production preflight
before the test stage's evidence passes.

263
00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:47.499
Does it pass anyway, fail on the
check that reads the test evidence,

264
00:15:47.499 --> 00:15:51.800
warn and let the operator decide,
or apply the plan regardless?

265
00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.964
The answer to check nine: the
production preflight fails,

266
00:15:58.964 --> 00:16:02.450
because production needs the
test stage's evidence first.

267
00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.459
That's the pilot ring, end to end:

268
00:16:04.459 --> 00:16:08.076
decided honestly about what
GCC does and doesn't state,

269
00:16:08.076 --> 00:16:12.335
logged before anything was
enforced, measured device by device,

270
00:16:12.335 --> 00:16:17.157
changed only through plans a second
person approved, enforced only when

271
00:16:17.157 --> 00:16:21.738
report-only was clean, and recorded
as evidence an assessor can check.

272
00:16:21.738 --> 00:16:22.875
Now it's your turn.

273
00:16:22.875 --> 00:16:27.138
Each chapter has a second lab in your
own test tenant, never production.

274
00:16:27.138 --> 00:16:31.226
Then go deeper: the workbook's
chapters and their narrated tours,

275
00:16:31.226 --> 00:16:34.312
the learning path, and
the implementation guide.

276
00:16:34.312 --> 00:16:35.480
Thanks for watching.
